WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.500
Cells from one patient carry receptors that grip LDL normally.

2
00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.980
Yet the LDL stays outside the cell.

3
00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.520
A receptor has to do more than bind.

4
00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.760
It must be gathered into the right patch of membrane, carried inside, let go of its

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.440
cargo, and come back.

6
00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:27.903
In mammalian cells, the steps run in order.

7
00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:33.983
Clathrin builds the coat, but clathrin binds neither membrane nor cargo.

8
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:41.123
Adaptors do that. An adaptor called AP2 binds a lipid in the inner leaflet of the

9
00:00:41.123 --> 00:00:46.263
membrane. Adaptors also read short sorting motifs in cargo tails.

10
00:00:47.063 --> 00:00:48.583
Then they recruit clathrin.

11
00:00:49.343 --> 00:00:52.843
The lattice grows, and the membrane curves into a pit.

12
00:00:53.723 --> 00:00:56.663
Dynamin forms a collar around the narrow neck.

13
00:00:57.503 --> 00:01:00.663
Using GTP, it drives the membrane to split.

14
00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:03.943
A chaperone then pulls the lattice apart.

15
00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:08.723
The uncoated vesicle fuses with an early endosome.

16
00:01:09.403 --> 00:01:13.023
Its lumen is mildly acid, roughly pH 6.

17
00:01:14.003 --> 00:01:16.923
For LDL, acid is the sorting signal.

18
00:01:17.703 --> 00:01:20.783
At this pH, LDL comes off its receptor.

19
00:01:21.743 --> 00:01:23.603
Receptor and cargo now part.

20
00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:26.253
The receptor goes back.

21
00:01:27.393 --> 00:01:32.353
Empty receptors collect in narrow tubules that pinch off and return to the plasma

22
00:01:32.353 --> 00:01:35.713
membrane. The LDL stays in the lumen.

23
00:01:37.193 --> 00:01:44.733
In human A431 cells, Rab5 was replaced by Rab7 on single endosomes carrying LDL.

24
00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:48.353
The authors read this as the compartment maturing.

25
00:01:49.393 --> 00:01:51.713
The LDL goes on to the lysosome.

26
00:01:51.713 --> 00:01:55.653
There, enzymes digest it and release cholesterol.

27
00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:58.947
These numbers are made up for practice.

28
00:02:00.127 --> 00:02:03.907
Cells bind labeled LDL in the cold, where nothing enters.

29
00:02:04.607 --> 00:02:07.807
Then they warm up, and a wash strips the surface.

30
00:02:08.947 --> 00:02:11.767
Normal cells bound 40 and took in 30.

31
00:02:12.287 --> 00:02:14.000
That is 75%.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:19.527
Strain X bound only 1.0, which is 2.5% of normal.

32
00:02:20.087 --> 00:02:21.387
That is a binding defect.

33
00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:25.907
Strain Y bound 38 but took in just 3.

34
00:02:26.447 --> 00:02:30.027
That is 7.9%. That is an entry defect.

35
00:02:30.917 --> 00:02:33.677
So the receptor makes a round trip.

36
00:02:34.357 --> 00:02:40.777
It binds at the surface, enters in a coated vesicle, lets go of LDL in the acid

37
00:02:40.777 --> 00:02:42.417
endosome, and returns.

38
00:02:43.557 --> 00:02:46.937
The LDL travels one way, to the lysosome.

39
00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:53.677
Different mutations break different steps, so a patient's cells can show which step

40
00:02:53.677 --> 00:02:55.917
failed. Keep this rule.

41
00:02:56.697 --> 00:03:01.357
A receptor must bind, enter, release, and return.
